1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERB: Far longer than any message.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: The reflector joins the 26 letters in 13 pairs, and turns the current round.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: So the machine works both ways: at the same setting, press the letter C, and the current runs backwards to A.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: Set a second machine the same way, type in the ciphertext, and out comes the message.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: No switch between coding and decoding at all.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: But there's a catch. Could a key ever light its own lamp?

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: Only if the current came back along the very wire it went in on.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: That would mean the reflector joining a letter to itself, and it never does.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERB: So no letter is ever enciphered as itself. Press A, and you get anything but A.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: We tried every key at every rotor position. Not one lit its own lamp.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: Couldn't the plugboard fix that?

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: No. It swaps a letter on the way in, and swaps it back on the way out.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERB: So how many ways could the army's Enigma actually be set up?

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERA: Let's count: three rotors from a box of five, and ten plugboard cables.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: Choosing the rotors and their order: five times four times three, or sixty ways.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: Their starting positions: 26 times 26 times 26. That's 17,576.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: And ten cables pairing up letters on the plugboard: about a hundred and fifty-one trillion ways.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERB: Multiply them, and that's nearly 159 quintillion settings, even before the rotors' ring settings.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: A number twenty-one digits long.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: At a million settings a second, trying them all would take about five million years.
